                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I think beating Thumper with all S ranks is one of the craziest, most obsessive, and most masochistic things I've ever done in my entire gaming career. And I'm not sure I'll ever want to do anything like it again in any other game.\n\nThumper is a very simple game. You're a metallic beetle moving along a single rail, trying to avoid obstacles and make it to the end of each level.\n\nA pretty straightforward and easy-to-grasp concept.\n\nWhere Thumper truly shines is in the exponential increase in complexity, speed, and difficulty. When it first came out, Thumper was described as a combination of a runner, a horror game, and a rhythm game. A runner because your little beetle \"runs\" toward the end of each level while avoiding obstacles; a rhythm game because the obstacles appear in sync with the music playing throughout the level; and a horror game because the sound effects and overall atmosphere genuinely give off that kind of vibe.\n\nThe game consists of 9 levels, each containing somewhere between 25 and 30 individual sections. Each section lasts anywhere from 20 seconds to a minute, depending on how good you are and how quick your reflexes are. To make it through each level, you need to hit the markers in sync with the music, smash through incoming obstacles, and take corners at exactly the right moment and in the right direction.\n\nWhen I first started playing Thumper, things were going pretty well. I was getting high ranks, A and S, throughout the first two or three levels, meaning I was hitting all the markers and keeping my beetle from taking damage while \"sliding\" along the track. My reflexes aren't particularly impressive, my sense of rhythm is so-so, but my brain really enjoys games where you can instantly restart an attempt whenever you mess something up. And that's how I came up with the optimistic idea of getting an S rank on every single level in Thumper.\n\nWhat I didn't anticipate was just how much faster the beetle would move as I progressed through the game, or how increasingly complex the obstacle patterns would become. And then learning advanced techniques became absolutely necessary just to survive a level, let alone complete it flawlessly... absolute insanity.\n\nThe music is genuinely great, and while everything feels intuitive at first, with obstacles and turns lining up with the expected drum beats, things get considerably harder once the later levels start introducing syncopation, polyrhythms, and rhythmic displacement. Suddenly, simply following the basic beat isn't enough anymore, because the actions start shifting to unexpected moments, and I constantly felt like I was falling out of rhythm. If any movement of my body or some random background noise distracted me for even a moment, my concentration would completely fall apart, and I'd have to start the entire section over if I wanted a flawless run. Even allowing myself to think about something else for a single second during a section meant starting all over again. I had to stay 100% focused at all times, with absolutely no room for my mind to wander.\n\nI really wish the game had some sort of statistics screen where I could see how many times I replayed certain levels. It took me 31 hours to fully complete the game and unlock every achievement. HLTB says it takes around 18 hours, so you can judge for yourselves how I stack up against the average gamer.\n\nThumper is a difficult game to recommend. But if you enjoy rhythm games and you're looking for a serious arcade challenge, Thumper can absolutely deliver, visually, mechanically, and audibly.",
